cartridge "TRENCHFIRE/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1984"

' TRENCHFIRE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Trenchfire" by Don Gibson, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette, March 1984 pp. 52-53 (Commodore 64 BASIC + machine language,
' and an all-machine-language VIC-20 version). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: you speed through a trench in an earthquake fault toward evil
' King Krypos' lair. His kamikaze drone ships fly at you out of the far end
' of the trench, with debris. Shoot them before they get too close, or
' dodge left or right. The waves never end. 3 ships, a new ship every 1000
' points (7 at most). The game ends when the last ship is hit.
'
' From the original (orig/trenchfire.64.bas, run in jsc64; the article):
' a predrawn trench whose colours are switched to make it move; your
' fighter at the bottom, steered left and right; one shot at a time,
' flying away down the trench toward its far end; drones that appear at
' the far end, grow as they come, each on its own slanting course, and
' pass by at the bottom; 25 points a drone; a waves of 12 + 3 x wave
' drones, with a bonus of 75 x wave when it is over (C64 lines 990, 6000);
' a new ship every 1000 points, at most 7. From the VIC-20 version: the
' four levels (beginner, intermediate as the default, advanced, expert),
' chosen on the title, and one level up for every 250 points.
'
' Liberties:
' - The trench is seen in perspective and its colour bands glide down a
'   few pixel lines at a time (the colour table of the predrawn trench is
'   rewritten, a third of it each frame, the bands stepping every 3
'   frames), instead of the C64's whole-ring colour swaps about 30 times
'   a second (measured in jsc64): a line changes colour at most every 21
'   frames (under 3 Hz), so nothing flashes.
' - Ships, shots and drones move along the trench in depth (one table of
'   progress -> screen row and width), so hits are judged in depth and
'   lane, not by sprite overlap.
' - Debris: rocks that roll at you on a straight course, 10 points each
'   (the article names debris; the C64 listing has only drones).
' - Kamikaze drones: from level 2 some drones steer toward your lane while
'   they are far away (the article's "kamikaze"); the rest keep the
'   C64's straight slanting courses. Many drones are aimed at the lane you
'   are in when they appear, so standing still does not work.
' - Levels 1-10: the title's level choice starts at 1, 3, 5 or 7; every
'   250 points adds one. Speed, number of drones at once and how often
'   they come grow with the level. Beginner drones take 3.3 s to arrive,
'   level 10 1.3 s (the C64 about 2 s at its first wave, measured).
' - A wave's bonus is counted in 75 points a frame.
' - No pause key (no keypad); no extra ship over 7 (the C64 gave 375
'   points instead). Original tune, sounds and drawings.
'
' Controls: either controller. Stick left/right steers the fighter,
' either button fires. On the title, up/down chooses a level, HOW TO PLAY
' (two pages: a button turns the page, 30 s idle goes back) or CREDITS;
' a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table; the title tune
' plays once. `set sprite count` always given.

